---
/**
 * The four site-wide links, at the bottom of the navigation rail.
 *
 * **This is the page's footer, moved — not a second one.** The hard rule is one minimalist footer
 * per page (`Footer.astro`, AI_INSTRUCTIONS → Architecture), and it still holds: `BaseLayout` is
 * told `dashNav` and skips rendering its own, so a dashboard has exactly one of these and it is
 * this. The move is what makes the rail possible at all — with the rail running the full height of
 * the viewport and the panel scrolling behind it, a footer at the END of the document is a footer
 * a seller reaches only after eight hundred product rows. Owner, 2026-08-23: *"הפעם הפוטר יורד
 * לתחתית הרצועה — תנאי שימוש, צור קשר, דיווח על תקלה ומתג שפה. בלי זה אל תבנה את זה בכלל."*
 *
 * Five links: the four he named, plus the returns policy he asked after — that one belongs here
 * more than it does on a shopper's page, because this page belongs to the business the policy
 * binds. What the page footer carries and this one does not is "open a store" (the reader already
 * has one) and the © line (chrome for a shopper's page, not for a working surface).
 *
 * "Report a fault" is `/contact?report=fault`, which is the link `ReportForm.astro` was built to
 * receive — it preselects the kind and captures the page it came from, so the seller types the
 * problem and nothing else.
 */
import CsrfField from '../CsrfField.astro';
import { getLang, getT } from '../../i18n/index.js';

const lang = getLang(Astro.cookies);
const t = getT(lang);
const nextLang = lang === 'he' ? 'en' : 'he';
---

<div class="dash-rail-foot">
  <a href="/terms">{t.footer.terms}</a>
  {/* Added on his question (2026-08-23: *"ביטולים והחזרות לא צריכים להיות גם בפוטר של הדשבורד
      מוכרים?"*) — and the answer is yes, more so here than on a shopper's page: this page belongs
      to the person the policy binds. It was left out only because the first pass took the four
      links he had listed and no more. */}
  <a href="/returns-policy">{t.footer.returns}</a>
  <a href="/contact">{t.footer.contact}</a>
  {/* `#report`, and it is the difference between two links and one (owner, 2026-08-23: *"בפוטר
      יצירת קשר ודיווח על תקלה זה אותו הקישור?!"* — he was right that they landed identically).
      `?report=fault` does real work: `ReportForm` opens with the fault kind chosen and the page
      it came from captured. But the query string is invisible, so both rows arrived at the top of
      the same page and the second looked like a mistake. The anchor is on the report section's own
      heading (contact.astro), so this one lands ON the form and the other lands on the page. */}
  <a href="/contact?report=fault#report">{t.dashboard.navReportFault}</a>
  {/* data-native-submit for the same reason the page footer's copy carries it: `/api/lang` answers
      303 and only a full navigation re-renders the page in the other language, so the dashboard's
      FormFallbackGuard must not intercept this one as an unsaved AJAX form. */}
  <form method="POST" action={`/api/lang?l=${nextLang}`} class="lang-toggle-form" data-native-submit>
    <CsrfField />
    <button type="submit" class="lang-toggle-btn" aria-label={t.nav.switchLangLabel}>
      {t.nav.switchLang}
    </button>
  </form>
</div>
